WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:05.640
Cloud workloads are the compute pieces that run applications and jobs. They may be virtual

00:00:05.860 --> 00:00:06.820
machines,

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:12.480
containers, serverless functions, managed compute services, batch jobs,

00:00:12.700 --> 00:00:14.940
or application platform components.

00:00:15.160 --> 00:00:18.120
The exact runtime changes from service to service,

00:00:18.340 --> 00:00:21.780
but the security questions are consistent: what code runs,

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:27.120
what image or template produced it, what identity does it use, what secrets can it reach,

00:00:27.340 --> 00:00:31.260
what network paths are open, and how is runtime activity monitored?

00:00:31.480 --> 00:00:37.080
Images and templates shape runtime security before the workload ever starts. A virtual

00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:40.020
machine image can include outdated packages,

00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:44.400
unnecessary services, default accounts, or weak configuration.

00:00:44.620 --> 00:00:49.900
A container image can include unused tools, vulnerable libraries, embedded secrets,

00:00:50.120 --> 00:00:51.800
or excessive permissions.

00:00:52.020 --> 00:00:57.380
A serverless package can include dependencies that nobody reviews. Secure workload design

00:00:57.600 --> 00:00:59.760
starts with maintained base images,

00:00:59.980 --> 00:01:04.300
minimal packages, approved templates, and repeatable build pipelines.

00:01:04.520 --> 00:01:09.240
Patching and vulnerability management still matter in the cloud. Managed services may

00:01:09.460 --> 00:01:11.300
reduce some operating system work,

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:16.480
but applications, dependencies, images, and runtime settings still need review.

00:01:16.700 --> 00:01:20.380
Teams should know how images are built, how often they are updated,

00:01:20.600 --> 00:01:24.200
how vulnerabilities are prioritized, and how fixes are rolled out.

00:01:24.420 --> 00:01:28.980
Immutable infrastructure can help because workloads are replaced from known images instead

00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:31.040
of manually repaired in place.

00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:36.700
That benefit only appears if the images and automation are maintained. Runtime permissions

00:01:36.920 --> 00:01:39.160
are just as important as software versions.

00:01:39.380 --> 00:01:44.020
A workload should have the identity it needs and no more. A function that reads one queue

00:01:44.240 --> 00:01:46.480
does not need broad administrator rights.

00:01:46.700 --> 00:01:51.340
A container that writes application logs does not need access to every secret.

00:01:51.560 --> 00:01:55.560
Secrets should be injected through approved mechanisms, rotated when needed,

00:01:55.780 --> 00:01:59.620
and kept out of images, environment dumps, and build logs.

00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:04.000
Workload identity should be reviewed the same way human access is reviewed.

00:02:04.220 --> 00:02:09.180
Runtime monitoring closes the loop. Teams need visibility into process behavior,

00:02:09.400 --> 00:02:13.160
deployment changes, failed starts, unusual network connections,

00:02:13.380 --> 00:02:17.940
unexpected privilege changes, and suspicious access to data or secrets.

00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:20.160
The goal is defensive awareness,

00:02:20.380 --> 00:02:25.180
not noise. Alerts should focus on signals that prompt useful review or remediation.

00:02:25.400 --> 00:02:30.520
Automation can improve workload security by making builds consistent, enforcing baselines,

00:02:30.740 --> 00:02:32.580
and reducing manual drift.

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:36.960
It can also spread mistakes quickly if templates are stale or pipelines are

00:02:37.180 --> 00:02:38.380
overprivileged.

00:02:38.600 --> 00:02:42.840
A healthy workload routine maintains base images, reviews dependencies,

00:02:43.060 --> 00:02:46.980
limits runtime identity, protects secrets, monitors behavior,

00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:49.280
and redeploys safely when risk changes.
